---
title: Updating Vesper
summary: Update one package, check the build, refresh your agent instructions and deploy, so editors get the new studio.
---

Vesper's packages share one version number, so there's one thing to update: `vespercms`. It brings the studio and the rest of Vesper with it, always at the same version.

{% callout type="note" %}
Vesper isn't on npm yet. This is how updating works once it is.
{% /callout %}

## Update the package

In your site's folder:

```sh
npm install vespercms@latest
```

Each release's changes are in the package's `CHANGELOG.md`, one plain sentence per change. Read them before you deploy, especially after a jump of more than one version.

Updating never touches your content. Vesper changes a file only when someone saves in the studio.

## Check the build

```sh
npx astro build
```

The build checks every entry against your schema, as before. It also checks every built page's accessibility structure and writes the report to `.vesper/a11y-report.json`. A newer Vesper can find something an older one missed, so look at the report even when the build passes.

## Refresh your agent instructions

If you use [`vesper agents`](/docs/agents/), run it again:

```sh
npx vesper agents
```

A new release can describe fields, tags or rules differently, so the section in `AGENTS.md` can change even when your schema didn't. In CI, `npx vesper agents --check` fails until you do.

## Deploy, so editors get the new studio

The studio at `/admin` is built with your site. Editors keep the studio they have until you deploy: on GitHub mode and the cloud, after your next push and build; for a site edited only locally, the next time you run `astro dev`.

The cloud itself is updated by Vesper, not by you. The relay you deployed for GitHub mode is your own copy of `templates/oauth-relay`, and updating `vespercms` doesn't change it.

## Astro and React

- **Astro:** Vesper supports the current major version of Astro and the one before it, 7 and 6 today. Before moving your site to a new major version, check that your Vesper version supports it.
- **React:** the studio needs React 19, the copy your site installs. With an older one, `astro dev`, and `astro build` when it builds `/admin`, stop with a message that says which version to install.
- **Optional packages:** `lucide` is needed only when `vesper.config.ts` names collection icons, and `sharp` is the copy Astro's own image service already uses. Update them as you would any other dependency.
